questions.answer:
A promise is pending, fulfilled, or rejected. Once it settles into fulfilled or rejected, it stays in that state permanently.

code:
const p = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
  setTimeout(() => resolve('done'), 100);
});
console.log(p);  // pending at first

real example:
Playwright actions return promises that are pending until the browser answers, which is why awaiting them keeps state deterministic.

allSettled waits for every promise and reports each result as fulfilled or rejected, while all rejects immediately on the first failure.

code:
const results = await Promise.allSettled(urls.map(u => fetch(u)));
results.forEach(r => console.log(r.status));

real example:
A multi-endpoint health check wants to log every failure, so allSettled is the right choice instead of all.

async/await makes promise-based code read like synchronous code. An async function returns a promise, and await unwraps a promise's value without nested callbacks.

code:
async function getUser() {
  const res = await fetch('/user');
  return res.json();
}

real example:
Every Playwright test step such as page.click and expect is awaited, which is why test bodies are marked async.

Wrap the awaited calls in try/catch, since an async function returns a rejected promise that the surrounding await or catch handles.

code:
async function run() {
  try {
    await riskyCall();
  } catch (err) {
    console.log('recovered:', err.message);
  }
}

real example:
Retry logic catches transient network errors on login and retries a bounded number of times instead of failing instantly.

Wrapping a callback-style function inside a Promise executor converts it to a promise API where resolve and reject are called by the callback.

code:
function wait(ms) {
  return new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));
}
await wait(1000);

real example:
Custom poll helpers in Playwright use this pattern to await arbitrary conditions after a delay.

Map the workload to promises, then await Promise.all or Promise.allSettled; the results array lines up with the input order.

code:
const totals = await Promise.all(
  orders.map(o => getTotal(o.id))
);

real example:
Fetching product details for a list of ids returns values in the same order, making per-index assertions simple.

Always attach catch or await the promise so rejections are observed. Unhandled rejections crash or warn loudly in Node and CI test runners.

code:
const p = risky();
p.catch(err => console.error('handled', err));
// or: await p;

real example:
Playwright reports unhandled errors as test failures, so route request failures must be awaited or caught to keep logs clean.
